// Coarse river sand: pale, uniform, rippled, and dramatic when it gets wet.
//
// The third shipped soil, and the one that exists because the other two cannot
// be tuned into it. `meadow_floor` and `compacted_loam` are both *cohesive*
// ground — they hold a crumb, they carry clods a centimetre and a half across,
// and one of them can crack. Sand does none of that, and every one of those
// differences is structural rather than a matter of colour.
//
// ## What actually makes sand read as sand
//
// Not the colour. A pale warm palette is the least of it: a photograph of dry
// loam at the right exposure is the same colour, and the two are never confused.
// Three things are:
//
//   **It has no aggregate structure.** Loam's coarsest band is five centimetres
//   of clustered clods, and clods are what the eye reads as *soil*. Sand's
//   coarsest band here is four millimetres. Between those two numbers is the
//   whole difference between ground that broke into lumps and ground that never
//   held together.
//
//   **It ripples.** Directional relief with coherence over a metre is the one
//   signal isotropic noise cannot fake — noise at ripple amplitude reads as a
//   rough plane, however carefully it is tuned. `RippleProfile` has been in the
//   schema since the beginning and no shipped profile had ever declared one, so
//   this is also the first thing to exercise that path.
//
//   **It is tonally flat.** Loam's region term moves the palette by half its
//   range, because a meadow floor genuinely is patchy — richer here, scuffed
//   there. Sand is sorted by water, which is a mixing process: a sand bar is
//   very nearly one colour over its whole length, and reproducing that
//   *uniformity* is what stops it reading as pale dirt.
//
// ## And why it is the material that shows moisture best
//
// Wet sand is the most dramatic wet response of any ground there is. A dry bar
// sits near an albedo of a quarter and the same sand at the waterline is under a
// tenth — a factor of three, where wet loam manages barely two. Sand's grains
// are large and near-transparent, so a water film changes almost all of the
// light path rather than a little of it.
//
// It also *smooths* more than any other soil. Water fills the space between
// grains completely, which is why a wet beach reflects the sky and a wet field
// does not: `saturation_flattening` is 0.78 here against loam's 0.45, and the
// ripples are suppressed on top of that.
(
    format: "ground-material",
    format_version: 1,
    material: (
        key: "river_sand",
        display_name: "River sand",

        optics: (
            // Quartz sand with a little iron. Linear light, like every other
            // palette here.
            //
            // ## Checked against the render, not only against the file
            //
            // The first version of this was authored by eye at a mid of 0.218
            // and looked correct in isolation. Measured off the plate beside
            // the compacted track it came back **1.18 times** the track's
            // luminance with a *lower* red-to-blue ratio — which is to say it
            // rendered as slightly lighter dirt, which is exactly what it reads
            // as. Dry quartz sand sits near an albedo of a third in red and is
            // conspicuously the warmest ground in any photograph that contains
            // it, so both numbers moved.
            //
            // The span is deliberately narrow — a factor of three and a half
            // between the low and high stops against loam's twenty-seven. That
            // narrowness *is* the material: sorted grains are all the same
            // grain, so there is nothing for a broad tonal field to describe.
            dry_palette: (
                // Rescaled with the other two — see `compacted_loam` for why
                // every palette in the set needed it. Still the brightest
                // ground here by a wide margin, which is what sand is.
                //
                // ## Halved with loam's, for the same measured reason
                //
                // Rendered on the comparison card these came back at **2.32
                // times** the mean luminance of the grass on the same plate,
                // where the reference photograph puts bare ground at 0.90 times
                // its own turf. Sand is the pale one and is meant to sit above
                // loam — but not at two and a third times a green sward, which
                // is a lit beach and reads as a blank card.
                //
                // Set to about 1.25 times the grass: clearly the palest ground
                // in the set, clearly still ground. Warmed a little as well,
                // because the ordering that matters is that sand is warmer than
                // loam is warmer than turf.
                low:  (0.03533, 0.02544, 0.01484),
                mid:  (0.07853, 0.05654, 0.03298),
                high: (0.12564, 0.09046, 0.05277),
            ),
            wet: (
                // A third of the dry mid, which is what a waterline actually
                // measures. Loam only manages a half, and the difference is
                // grain size: sand's grains are large enough that a film
                // changes nearly the whole light path.
                // A third of the dry mid, and warmer than it: a water film
                // absorbs blue harder than red, so wet sand is not merely
                // darker sand — it moves along the hue axis as well, and doing
                // only the darkening is what makes wet ground read as ground in
                // shadow.
                //
                // Neutral as well as dark, like the other two. Sand keeps a
                // little more of its warmth than a cohesive soil does — the
                // grains are large and the film is thin over them — but not
                // the factor of three the dry palette carries.
                // Thirty-five per cent of the dry mid — a harder darkening than
                // loam's forty-five, which is the physical claim this profile
                // has always made and now survives the palette moving under it.
                wet_mid: (0.02931, 0.01935, 0.01055),
                // Glossier than any other ground here. Wet sand reflects the
                // sky; wet loam does not — and it stays the glossier of the two
                // now that both have come up, because that ordering is the
                // physical claim and 0.10 against 0.12 barely made it.
                roughness_wet: 0.34,
                // Water fills the space between grains almost completely. The
                // single largest figure in the shipped set, against loam's
                // 0.45, and it is why a wet bar looks poured rather than
                // heaped.
                saturation_flattening: 0.58,
                film_ior: 1.333,
            ),
            // Wide, and wider than loam's, which is the opposite of what the
            // first version had. Sand is a heap of *individual* quartz grains
            // with faces on them, so the brightest parts of a dry bar are
            // grains catching the sun at a specular angle while the shaded
            // pockets between them scatter diffusely. Clamping the whole
            // surface to 0.90–0.99 removed every one of those catches, and what
            // came back was a matte card that read as dried mud.
            //
            // The bright end still stays under loam's, because a *crest* of
            // sand is loose grains rather than a packed crust.
            //
            // ## And the low end was far too low once the sun was aimed
            //
            // 0.42 was chosen while the sun pointed away from the camera, where
            // nothing specular could be seen at any roughness. With the lobe
            // aimed (see `RenderSettings::sun_azimuth`) the same number turned a
            // dry bar into a sheet of wet-looking plastic — which is exactly the
            // reading the wet state has to own. Dry sand catches the odd grain;
            // it does not shine.
            roughness_dry: (0.72, 0.97),
            // Quartz.
            ior: 1.544,

            // Tonally flat, which is the point. Half of loam's region strength
            // over one and a half times its wavelength: a bar varies over
            // metres, not over hand's widths.
            // ## "Tonally flat" was read too literally the first time
            //
            // A sand bar *is* uniform compared to a meadow floor, and the first
            // version of this profile turned that into region 0.26 and patch
            // 0.38 — so nothing in the colour varied at any scale a viewer
            // resolves. Combined with a single relief band at four millimetres
            // and one ripple frequency, the only feature left on the surface
            // was the ripple, and what rendered was corduroy: a fabric, not a
            // ground.
            //
            // Sand is uniform in *hue* and not in value. A real bar is covered
            // in scour marks, damp patches, drift lines and darker heavy-mineral
            // streaks, all of them the same colour and none of them the same
            // brightness. So the region term stays modest — that is the honest
            // part — and the patch term goes up hard.
            // Both pulled back with loam's, and for the same reason: at 0.72 the
            // patch term was the loudest thing on the surface, and a sand bar is
            // not mottled. What it has is scour and ripple, which are now deep
            // enough to be seen. The patch term stays the larger of the two here
            // — drift lines and damp streaks are real, and they are what stops
            // a uniform hue reading as a swatch — but it no longer competes with
            // the form.
            region_wavelength_m: 2.4,
            region_strength: 0.26,
            patch_wavelength_m: 0.085,
            patch_strength: 0.40,
            // Fine and even. Loam's grain is 0.55 and describes crumb; this
            // describes actual grains, which at any framing this project
            // renders at are below a pixel and belong in the BRDF rather than
            // in the colour.
            // And the grain goes with it. This is the term that puts something
            // *between* the ripples, which is the difference between a rippled
            // surface and a ribbed one.
            grain_strength: 0.72,
        ),

        structure: (
            // Three bands, none of them a clod. The coarsest is four
            // millimetres — a tenth of loam's — because sand has no aggregate
            // above grain scale at all. Everything larger on a real bar is a
            // ripple, and ripples are declared separately below where their
            // *direction* can be stated.
            // ## And these were far too shallow to be seen
            //
            // Exported and measured, bare sand came back at **2.0 mm
            // peak-to-peak** over a twelve-centimetre window, mean slope 5.6
            // degrees. A 35-degree sun cannot shadow that and neither can it
            // shade it: what rendered was a flat card carrying a colour field,
            // which is exactly what it looked like.
            //
            // Sand is genuinely gentler than soil — it has no aggregates and its
            // angle of repose is what it is — so it does not want loam's flanks.
            // What it wants is *depth*: scour hollows a centimetre and a half
            // deep rather than three millimetres, and ripples that stand tall
            // enough to shade their own lee.
            bands: [
                // Scour: broad, shallow, irregular hollows a hand across, of
                // the kind water leaves when it drops its load unevenly. New,
                // and it is what stops the ripples being the only structure —
                // a single relief frequency over a single tonal field is a
                // woven fabric however carefully each is tuned.
                //
                // Clustered, so the scoured areas group rather than covering
                // the bar evenly.
                (wavelength_m: 0.1400, amplitude_m: 0.0180, shape: "Rounded", compaction_response: 0.35, clustered: true),
                (wavelength_m: 0.0340, amplitude_m: 0.0070, shape: "Rounded", compaction_response: 0.35, clustered: true),
                // Coarse grains and the odd shell fragment.
                (wavelength_m: 0.0090, amplitude_m: 0.0016, shape: "Angular", compaction_response: 0.30, clustered: false),
                (wavelength_m: 0.0040, amplitude_m: 0.00090, shape: "Angular", compaction_response: 0.30, clustered: false),
                (wavelength_m: 0.0012, amplitude_m: 0.00032, shape: "Angular", compaction_response: 0.18, clustered: false),
            ],
            // Nothing clusters. A clump of sand is a thing that has stopped
            // being sand, and leaving the clustering on would put loam's
            // hand's-width lumpiness back into a material defined by not
            // having it.
            cluster_wavelength_m: 0.8,
            // The scour band clusters; nothing finer does. A clump of sand is
            // a thing that has stopped being sand.
            cluster_strength: 0.55,
            // Down to the second scour band. Sand has no aggregate hierarchy to
            // resolve, so this is a shallower request than loam's — it buys the
            // three-centimetre hollows between the ripples and stops there.
            mesh_floor_m: Some(0.0340),
            // Almost none. This is the number that says the material cannot
            // hold a shape, and it is why there is no crack profile below: a
            // material with no cohesion has nothing to crack.
            cohesion: 0.06,
        ),

        // ## Ripples, on the second attempt, and short crested
        //
        // The first attempt declared a `RippleProfile` and three renders came
        // back as corduroy, then brushed burlap, then woven canvas. Lowering
        // the amplitude, trebling the meander and adding a patchiness field
        // each produced a slightly different fabric, and the profile shipped
        // with `ripples: None` for a while because of it.
        //
        // The defect was in the *solver*, not in these numbers. A ripple drawn
        // as a continuous wave is a weave however it is tuned, because the one
        // thing a real ripple field has that a sine does not is termination:
        // crests run for a few times their own wavelength, die out, and a new
        // one starts offset from it, with smooth ground between. `ripple_height`
        // now carries that, so a wavelength that used to cover a whole plate
        // covers about half of it in runs a third of a metre long.
        //
        // The envelope is what a river bar actually measures: eight-centimetre
        // crest spacing, a millimetre and a half of relief, and coverage near
        // a half.
        ripples: Some((
            wavelength_m: 0.080,
            // ## A millimetre and a half was an over-correction
            //
            // The first attempt used four, which put more relief in the ripple
            // than in everything else on the surface combined — a ripple is a
            // texture *over* a ground, not the ground — and what came back was
            // corduroy. So it went to one and a half.
            //
            // At one and a half it is invisible. The slope of an 80 mm wave
            // standing 1.5 mm is **three degrees**; under a 35-degree sun that
            // is not a ripple field, it is a plane. The corduroy was never the
            // amplitude's fault — it was the continuous wavefront, which
            // `ripple_height` now terminates.
            //
            // Twelve millimetres is what a river bar measures, and with the
            // scour bands beneath it now carrying real depth the ripple is once
            // again a texture over a ground rather than the only thing on it.
            // It puts the crest slope near 25 degrees, which under a 35-degree
            // sun is a ripple that shades its own lee without casting — which is
            // what a ripple does.
            amplitude_m: 0.0120,
            direction_deg: 35.0,
            crest_sharpness: 0.42,
            asymmetry: 0.45,
            meander_m: 0.055,
            // Wet sand does not ripple: the water holds the grains where they
            // are. Nearly total, so their disappearance is one more thing that
            // reads as wetness.
            wetness_suppression: 0.88,
        )),

        // None, and deliberately. Cracking is a clay behaviour — it needs
        // cohesion for the shrinking sheet to hold together long enough to
        // part. Sand shrinks by draining, which moves no grains relative to
        // each other, so a cracked sand is a thing nobody has photographed.
        cracks: None,

        scatter: (
            // A great deal of grit and almost no pebbles, which is what sorting
            // means: a river drops its coarse load before it drops its fine
            // one, so a sand bar is sand.
            grit_per_m2: 220.0,
            pebble_per_m2: 0.12,
            fragment_radius_m: (0.0018, 0.010),
        ),

        // Nearly nothing grows in it. Higher than the track's zero, because a
        // river bar does carry the odd tuft where silt has collected, and
        // making it exactly zero would forbid that in every document at once
        // rather than letting one say so.
        vegetation_affinity: 0.04,
    ),
)
